{
  "summary": "Defender's virus protection is switched on and guarded against tampering, but the computer is not keeping up to date. Its virus definitions are 40 days old, and the newest Windows update is 95 days old. Getting updates flowing again is the first thing to do. After that, take a quick look at the extra account on the list to make sure you recognize it.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Windows and Defender are not getting updates",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PATCH-STALE:03036687",
        "DEF-SIGNATURES-OLD:cd772dcf"
      ],
      "explanation": "These two findings point to the same underlying problem: this computer isn't receiving updates. The newest Windows update installed is from 2026-08-20, which is 95 days ago. Microsoft releases security fixes every month, so about three months of fixes for known weaknesses are likely missing. Defender's virus definitions are also 40 days old. Defender itself is running normally, with real-time protection on and tamper protection enabled. However, it can only recognize threats it has definitions for, so it may miss newer malware. Both findings are rated high, and that is fair, because together they leave the computer open to well-known attacks. The report shows the fix for each finding; check both after updating to confirm they are resolved."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Confirm you recognize the account <computer>\\<account-2>",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The scan found a local account, <computer>\\<account-2>, that has full control over the computer. This is often perfectly normal. Many people keep a separate account like this on purpose, and that is good practice. It matters only if you don't recognize the account, because an unfamiliar account with this level of control can be a sign that someone else got in. This finding is rated low, so a quick check is enough. If you know what this account is, no action is needed; if you don't, see the fix shown in the report."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
